          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-59b432fefb8e03fe\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Boris directly presents his love for Natasha as an already-known emotional state. The clause is primarily an avowal or reminder and therefore strongly truth-conditional. Its place in a negotiation about waiting four years and eventually asking for her hand gives it slight undertaking-related force, but the explicit commitment occurs in the continuation rather than in the highlighted clause itself. Nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is limited T/P ambiguity because the avowal introduces a courtship arrangement, but the highlighted words themselves do not clearly enact the subsequent promise.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Boris directly addresses Natasha in narrated dialogue; the utterance is represented verbatim within the novel.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Natasha has just kissed Boris and prompts a declaration of reciprocal love. Boris responds with a qualified avowal—“you know that I love you, but…”—then restrains immediate intimacy and proposes waiting four years before asking for her hand. Natasha treats the ensuing exchange as a binding romantic settlement, but the highlighted clause chiefly states or reaffirms Boris’s feeling rather than itself making that commitment. His measured response also weighs against expressive/reflexive force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Наташа… вы знаете, что я люблю вас, но…” (“Natasha… you know that I love you, but…”)\",\"supports\":\"The complement “I love you” explicitly presents Boris’s loving state as true, while “you know” frames it as an avowal or reminder.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Natasha interrupts with “Вы влюблены в меня?” and Boris answers “Да, влюблен” (“Are you in love with me?” — “Yes, I am”).\",\"supports\":\"The immediate paraphrase and confirmation strongly establish truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Boris continues: “Еще четыре года… Тогда я буду просить вашей руки” (“Four more years… Then I will ask for your hand”).\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs within a courtship negotiation and has slight performative pressure, although the explicit future undertaking is expressed separately.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Boris speaks after waiting and then qualifies the declaration with “but”; the narration does not describe blurting, involuntary speech, or words escaping him.\",\"supports\":\"The scene supports a controlled avowal rather than expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Natasha asks whether it is settled forever, and Boris answers that it is settled.\",\"supports\":\"A relational undertaking emerges in the broader exchange, clarifying why P has slight support without making it the main force of the highlighted clause.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately represents the core utterance: it is dominantly a truth-conditional avowal with slight commitment-related force from its immediate use in courtship negotiation and no independent evidence for expressive/reflexive force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
